{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Instructor Implementation Toolkit","title":"Accessibility Review Workflow","description":"AIL 606 textbook section on running a WCAG-anchored accessibility review of a prototype: mapping elements to WCAG 2.2 success criteria, prioritizing fixes by depth of exclusion, and adding redundant cues for learner variability, with reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-01","statement":"Map each element of a prototype screen to a named WCAG 2.2 success criterion (1.1.1, 1.4.1, 1.4.3, 2.1.1, 2.2.1) rather than judging by appearance"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-02","statement":"Prioritize accessibility fixes by depth of exclusion, fixing full lock-outs before strain"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-03","statement":"Add redundant cues (color plus label plus icon) consistent with UDL learner variability without raising load for other learners"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-04","statement":"Judge an AI-drafted alt text against WCAG 1.1.1 (does it name what the image shows and why it matters) before accepting it"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit","accessibility","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"05","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/08/04"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Accessibility Review Workflow\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Accessibility, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/08-05-accessibility-review-workflow.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Accessibility Review Workflow: Accessibility, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAccessibility is not a finishing pass you run on a polished screen; it is a property of whether a learner can perceive, operate, and understand the material at all. A storyboard frame that looks immaculate can be entirely closed to a screen-reader user, unreadable to someone with low vision, or unusable by a learner who cannot distinguish red from green. An accessibility review workflow is the disciplined procedure that replaces \"looks clean\" with a checkable list of who is currently excluded and why.\n\nThis matters because accessibility failures are invisible to the person who built the screen. The designer who can see the diagram, read the gray text, and notice the red highlight has no native signal that any of those choices excludes someone. A workflow anchored to explicit criteria is what makes the invisible exclusion visible, and turns a vague sense that \"we should be accessible\" into specific, fixable defects tied to specific learners.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Map each element of a prototype screen to a named WCAG 2.2 success criterion (1.1.1, 1.4.1, 1.4.3, 2.1.1, 2.2.1) rather than judging by appearance.\n- Prioritize accessibility fixes by depth of exclusion, fixing full lock-outs before strain.\n- Add redundant cues (color plus label plus icon) consistent with UDL learner variability without raising load for other learners.\n- Judge an AI-drafted alt text against WCAG 1.1.1 (does it name what the image shows and why it matters) before accepting it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer presents a finished prototype screen at a critique: clean typography, a labeled diagram, instructions in elegant light-gray text, and a red frame marking the next step. The room nods; it looks done. Then someone runs it through a screen reader, which announces only \"image\" where the diagram should be, and checks the gray text, which measures 2.9 to 1 against the background, well below the 4.5 to 1 floor. The \"next\" cue is color alone, invisible to a color-blind learner. Three learners were excluded by a screen everyone in the room called finished.\n\nThe case makes the workflow's whole point: \"looks clean\" is a statement about the reviewer's own perception, not about access. As you read, notice how every finding in a real review is tied to a named criterion and a named excluded learner, which is what separates an accessibility review from an aesthetic reaction.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn accessibility review workflow is a repeatable procedure that maps each element of a screen to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at conformance level AA and produces a prioritized, checkable list of defects. WCAG organizes everything under four principles, abbreviated POUR: content must be Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, and Robust. Each principle decomposes into testable success criteria with identifiers, and those identifiers are what make a review auditable rather than impressionistic. As of 2026, WCAG 2.2 (a W3C Recommendation, last revised December 2024) is the operative standard: WCAG 3.0 remains a W3C Working Draft, not a finished standard, and does not deprecate WCAG 2. The 4.5:1 and 3:1 contrast floors and the success-criterion identifiers used in this section (1.1.1, 1.4.1, 1.4.3, 2.1.1, 2.2.1) are WCAG 2.2 success criteria, with AA conformance setting the contrast minimums the review relies on.\n\nThree of those criteria recur in instructional media often enough to memorize:\n\n- **1.1.1 Non-text content.** Every meaningful image needs a text alternative. A diagram without alt text is silence to a screen-reader user. The alt text should convey what the image means in context, not merely that an image exists.\n- **1.4.3 Contrast (minimum).** Normal text needs a contrast ratio of at least 4.5 to 1 against its background (3 to 1 for large text). Light-gray-on-white routinely fails this and excludes low-vision learners.\n- **1.4.1 Use of color.** Information conveyed by color must also be available another way. \"The red frame is next\" fails; \"the frame labeled Next, with an arrow\" passes.\n\nTwo principles govern how you act on findings. First, prioritize by depth of exclusion: a missing alt text fully locks out a screen-reader user, so it outranks a marginal contrast miss that merely strains a sighted reader. Second, born-accessible beats retrofit. Universal Design for Learning treats variability as the norm, so designing redundant cues from the start (color plus label plus icon) is cheaper and cleaner than bolting accommodations onto a finished screen.\n\nMatch each WCAG success criterion to the failure it names and the learner it excludes. These three recur often enough to memorize.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"1.1.1 Non-text content\",\"definition\":\"A diagram with no alt text announces only image to a screen-reader user; fix with one sentence naming what it shows and why\"},{\"term\":\"1.4.3 Contrast (minimum)\",\"definition\":\"Light-gray text at 2.9 to 1 falls below the 4.5 to 1 floor and excludes low-vision learners; raise the ratio\"},{\"term\":\"1.4.1 Use of color\",\"definition\":\"A color-only action cue (the red frame is next) is invisible to a color-blind learner; add a label and icon\"},{\"term\":\"2.1.1 Keyboard\",\"definition\":\"A drag-and-drop interaction with no keyboard alternative excludes anyone who cannot operate a mouse\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n| WCAG criterion | Failure on the screen | Who is excluded | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1.1.1 Non-text content | Diagram has no alt text | Screen-reader users | One-sentence alt naming what and why |\n| 1.4.3 Contrast | Text at 2.9 to 1 | Low-vision learners | Raise to at least 4.5 to 1 |\n| 1.4.1 Use of color | Action cue is color-only | Color-blind learners | Add label plus icon as redundant cues |\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one concrete accessibility review of a single prototype screen, a narrated storyboard frame with an embedded diagram and a \"Next\" button.\n\n**Before (the polished-but-inaccessible screen).** The screen looked finished: clean typography, a labeled diagram, instructions in light gray on white. A structured WCAG-anchored pass found three concrete blockers. (a) The diagram had no alt text, so a screen-reader user reached \"image\" with no content. (b) The instruction text measured a 2.9:1 contrast ratio against the background, below the 4.5:1 minimum for normal text. (c) The only cue for the required action was color (\"the red frame is next\"), unusable for a color-blind learner.\n\n1. **Review against criteria, not impression.** \"Looks clean\" is not an accessibility finding. Each blocker is tied to a specific WCAG success criterion (1.1.1 non-text content, 1.4.3 contrast, 1.4.1 use of color), so the review produces a checkable list rather than a vibe.\n2. **Fix in priority order by who is excluded.** The missing alt text fully excludes a screen-reader user, so it is fixed first: a one-sentence alt description naming what the diagram shows and why it matters. Contrast is raised to 4.6:1. The color-only cue gains a text label plus an arrow icon so the action is conveyed three ways.\n3. **Confirm the fix does not add load for everyone else.** By **UDL learner variability**, the redundant cue (color plus label plus icon) supports varied learners without crowding the screen, because the existing layout had room.\n\n**After (the screen as an accessible artifact).** Three named WCAG blockers closed: alt text added (1.1.1), contrast 2.9:1 raised to 4.6:1 (1.4.3), action cue no longer color-only (1.4.1). The change is auditable: a reviewer can map each fix to its success criterion and to the specific learner who was previously excluded, justified by **UDL learner variability**.\n\nDecide the fix order by depth of exclusion. Predict which defect to close first before each reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Ordering accessibility fixes by who is most excluded\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Three blockers are open: missing alt text on the diagram (1.1.1), text at 2.9 to 1 contrast (1.4.3), and a color-only next cue (1.4.1). Which do you fix first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The missing alt text (1.1.1)\",\"The contrast (1.4.3)\",\"Whichever is fastest to type\",\"The color cue (1.4.1)\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"The missing alt text. It fully locks out a screen-reader user (the diagram simply does not exist for them), so a total exclusion outranks the contrast strain.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You fix the color-only cue. A reviewer suggests just using a brighter, more saturated red. Does that satisfy 1.4.1?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"No; the information must exist in a non-color channel\",\"Yes; brighter red is easier to see\",\"Only for large text\",\"Yes, if contrast also passes\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"No. Brightening color is still color. 1.4.1 requires a second channel, so you add a text label and an arrow icon alongside the color.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final order and audit: alt text (1.1.1) first as a total exclusion, then contrast raised 2.9 to 1 -> 4.6 to 1 (1.4.3), then the color cue carried in three channels (1.4.1). Each fix maps to a criterion and a named excluded learner, and the redundant cue fits because the layout had whitespace.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nReview against named criteria, not against your own impression, and fix in the order of who is most fully excluded. An accessibility finding that cannot be tied to a WCAG criterion is an opinion; a fix that does not say which learner it unblocks has no priority.\n\nRun every screen through three checks:\n\n1. **Criterion check:** For each potential issue, which specific success criterion does it violate (1.1.1, 1.4.3, 1.4.1, or another)? If you cannot name one, you have a preference, not a defect.\n2. **Exclusion-depth check:** Does this issue fully lock a learner out (no alt text, so the diagram simply does not exist for them) or merely strain them (slightly low contrast)? Full exclusions are fixed first.\n3. **Redundancy check:** After the fix, is the same information available through at least two channels (color and label and icon), and does the added cue fit without crowding the screen?\n\nA screen that passes all three is accessible by evidence; one that only \"looks fine\" has not been reviewed, only admired.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An automated checker reports zero errors on a screen, so a designer marks it accessible. What does the workflow say about this?\" explanation=\"Automated tools catch measurable issues like contrast ratios and missing alt attributes, but they cannot judge whether alt text is meaningful, whether reading order makes sense, or whether a color cue has a real alternative. The checker is one input, not the verdict; the workflow still needs human judgment against the criteria.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A clean checker is one input, not a verdict; meaningful alt text and real color alternatives still need human judgment.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A clean automated report certifies full WCAG compliance.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Automated checkers replace the need for named criteria.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Zero errors means every learner is included.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misread is that a clean, professional look implies accessibility. Visual polish and access are independent properties; a beautifully typeset screen can fail every relevant criterion, and a plain one can pass them all. Treating aesthetics as evidence of access is how excluded learners stay invisible.\n\nA second misread is that an automated checker certifies accessibility. Automated tools catch contrast ratios and missing alt attributes, but they cannot judge whether alt text is meaningful, whether reading order makes sense, or whether a color cue has a real alternative. The workflow needs human judgment against the criteria, with the checker as one input, not the verdict.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nRun the workflow on the critique screen from the opening. Start with Perceivable, because perception failures are total exclusions. The diagram triggers 1.1.1: add a one-sentence alt that says what the diagram shows and why it matters here, not \"diagram of process.\" The gray instructions measure 2.9 to 1, failing 1.4.3; raise the text to 4.6 to 1.\n\nMove to Operable and Understandable. The \"next\" action is signaled by the red frame alone, failing 1.4.1; add a text label and an arrow so the cue lives in three channels. Now check the fix against UDL: redundant cues must not crowd everyone else. Because the layout had whitespace, color plus label plus icon fits without raising load for sighted learners.\n\nClose the loop with priority and audit. The alt text was fixed first because it fully excluded a screen-reader user, ahead of the contrast strain. Each fix is logged against its criterion (1.1.1, 1.4.3, 1.4.1) and the learner it unblocks. The walkthrough is complete when a reviewer can map every change to a criterion and a person, which is the difference between an accessible screen and one that merely got prettier.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this section looks at any screen and mentally walks POUR, asking at each principle \"who cannot perceive, operate, or understand this, and which criterion names that gap?\" That habit converts a vague worry about inclusion into specific, addressable defects.\n\nRead any prototype against these four questions:\n\n1. For each non-text element, is there a meaningful text alternative, or only a placeholder (1.1.1)?\n2. Does any text fall below the contrast floor for its size (1.4.3)?\n3. Is any required information carried by color alone, with no second channel (1.4.1)?\n4. When you fix the most-excluding issue first, does the redundant cue you add fit without raising load for other learners?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong accessibility artifact reports each defect by its WCAG criterion, the learner it excluded, the fix, and the post-fix measurement (for example, contrast raised from 2.9 to 1 to 4.6 to 1). A reviewer should be able to map every change to a criterion and a person, and to see that the fixes were ordered by depth of exclusion.\n\nA strong rationale sounds like this: \"I added alt text first (1.1.1) because the missing alternative fully excluded a screen-reader user, ahead of the contrast strain. I rejected the suggestion to convey the next step with a brighter red instead of adding a label, because brightening color does not satisfy 1.4.1; the information must exist in a non-color channel.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the workflow from a single static screen to an interactive simulation with drag-and-drop and timed feedback. The POUR criteria still hold, but Operable now dominates: drag-and-drop must have a keyboard-only alternative (criterion 2.1.1), and timed interactions must be adjustable for learners who need more time (criterion 2.2.1), constraints that a static screen never raised. The faithful transfer is to extend the review to the criteria the new interaction introduces rather than reusing the static checklist. Porting only the alt-text-and-contrast pass would certify a simulation that is unusable to anyone who cannot operate a mouse under time pressure.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/08-05-accessibility-review-workflow-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"08.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 08.05. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_accessibility_review_workflow\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Accessibility Review Workflow, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit\", \"accessibility\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- W3C (2024). Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. W3C Recommendation, 12 December 2024. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_accessibility_review_workflow","stem":"A reviewer says 'this screen looks clean, so it is accessible.' What is the strongest correction?","options":["Agree, since clean layouts are usually accessible.","Check the screen against named WCAG criteria such as alt text, contrast, and color-only cues; appearance is not a finding.","Add more whitespace until it looks even cleaner.","Ask a colleague whether they also think it looks clean."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accessibility findings come from specific success criteria, not from how polished the screen appears.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_clean_means_accessible","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["accessibility"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"The only cue for the required next action is 'the red frame is next.' Which WCAG issue does this raise?","options":["It is fine because red is easy to see.","Conveying the action by color alone fails use-of-color (1.4.1); add a text label and an icon.","It only affects the font size.","It is a contrast problem, so just darken the red."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Color alone excludes color-blind learners; the action must be conveyed in more than one channel.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_color_only_cue","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A reviewer uses AI to draft alt text for a content-bearing diagram. What is the strongest boundary?","options":["Accept the AI alt text unread because it is grammatical.","Use the AI draft as a starting point, then check it names what the diagram shows and why it matters before accepting.","Leave the alt text empty since the image is decorative.","Ask AI only to make the alt text longer."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI can draft alt text, but the reviewer must verify it conveys the diagram's content and purpose (WCAG 1.1.1).","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_decorative_alt_assumption","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-02","ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_accessibility_review_workflow_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Three blockers are found: missing alt text, 2.9:1 contrast, and a color-only action cue. Which fix should go first and why?","options":["Contrast, because it is the easiest to change.","Alt text, because the missing description fully excludes a screen-reader user from the content.","The color cue, because color is the most noticeable.","Any order, since all three are equally urgent."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Prioritize by who is fully excluded; a screen-reader user gets nothing from an undescribed content image.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_decorative_alt_assumption","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-05-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_clean_means_accessible","pattern":"visual_polish_mistaken_for_access","description":"Believing a clean, well-typeset screen is therefore accessible","trigger":"review judges the screen by appearance instead of named WCAG criteria","feedback":"Check against specific success criteria (alt text, contrast, color cues); looking clean is not an accessibility finding.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_color_only_cue","pattern":"color_as_sole_action_cue","description":"Conveying a required action using color alone","trigger":"the only cue for the next action is a color, with no label or icon","feedback":"Add a text label or icon so the action is conveyed in more than one channel (WCAG 1.4.1).","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_5_decorative_alt_assumption","pattern":"content_image_left_without_alt","description":"Assuming a content-bearing diagram needs no alt text","trigger":"an informative image has empty or missing alt text","feedback":"Give content images a one-sentence alt description naming what they show and why it matters (WCAG 1.1.1).","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"79f3bf62fe1a5cbbb6be157b3d1181234cd952c4d182d04cc0cc824b7de7565f","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}